@Contacting the Author
@Where can I get the sources?
#Where can I get the sources?

The current sources of HU-Prolog can be found on

        ftp.informatik.hu-berlin.de [141.20.20.38]
        Login name:  anonymous
        Password:    <your own mail address>

in the directory

        /pub/unix/languages/huprolog

If not, contact me.

@Contacting the Author
#What do I do about a bug?
1) This program has no bugs! It is always, without exception,
   an optical illusion. :-))
2) If bugs really should occur, it is the user's fault. :-)
3) If it is not the user's fault, please let me know.
   Since in case of doubt I will always insist on point 1)
   or 2), a short Prolog program that reproduces the bug is
   certainly very valuable.
   Short means that I can understand it without having to invest
   a week of time (it should fit on one screen page).

@Contacting the Author
#Whom may I thank for this masterpiece?
HU-Prolog was developed at the Humboldt University of Berlin
(hence the name :-)) From 1987 to 89 HU-Prolog had
three fathers:

    Christian Horn, Mirko Dziadzka and Matthias Horn.

In 1990 (almost) the whole system was rewritten from scratch by
Mirko Dziadzka, to make the sources understandable again and to
document them. Based on this version, Christian Horn and Mirko
Dziadzka (each on his own) have continued the development of
HU-Prolog since then. At regular intervals we try to unify
our versions.
(the emphasis is on `try')

