#,/2
','(Goal1,Goal2) :-
        Goal1,
        Goal2.
,/2 is transparent with respect to !/0
@!/0
#:-/2
When defining clauses, :-/2 serves as the separator between
clause head and clause body. In HU-Prolog, :-/2 also acts
as a predicate:

':-'(X,Y) :-
        assert((X :- Y)).

@assert/1
#:=/2
The second argument of :=/2 is evaluated. Any global variables
and user-defined functions that have been defined are taken
into account.
The result of this evaluation is assigned to the first argument
of :=/2. If that is a Prolog variable or a number, :=/2
behaves like is/2.
If it is any other term, it is interpreted as a global
variable, and the value is assigned to it.
A term marked with the prefix operator ` is not evaluated
but taken over unchanged.

Example:
?- status := `none.
?- X := status.
X = none
?- counter(17) := 0.
?- counter(17) := counter(17) + 1.
?- X := counter(17).
X = 1
?-listing.
counter(1,17).
@Global Variables and User-Defined Functions
@Arithmetic
@is/2
@Known Bugs of :=/2
#Known Bugs of :=/2
If the first argument of :=/2 is a Prolog term with
free variables, and the result of evaluating the second
argument also contains free variables, the result may
be wrong. In this case a warning is generated.

In general, the handling of unbound variables in :=/2
is not quite right.
#;/2
    (X ; _) :- X.
    (_ ; Y) :- Y.

;/2 is transparent with respect to !/0.
@!/0
#</2
:Arithmetic Comparison Operators
#=../2
The predicate =../2 is used for the analysis and synthesis of
terms. The second argument is a list of the components of the
first argument.

Example:
?- 7 =.. X.
    X = [7].
?- hello(world) =.. X.
    X = [hello | world]
?- X =.. [this, is, [a , test], Y].
    X = this(is, [a, test], Y)
?- X =.. [Y].
        ERROR .....     % not sufficiently instantiated
@functor/3
@arg/3
#=/2
:Unifiability
#\=/2
:Unifiability
@not/1
#=:=/2
:Arithmetic Comparison Operators
#=\=/2
:Arithmetic Comparison Operators
#=</2
:Arithmetic Comparison Operators
#==/2
==/2 checks whether its two arguments are identical terms.
No unification is performed.

Examples:
?- a == a.
    yes
?- a(X) == a(X).
    yes
?- a(X) == a(Y).
    no
@\==/2
@=/2
@=:=/2
#\==/2
Definition:
    X  \== Y :-
        not X == Y.
@==/2
@not/1
