#abolish/2
abolish(Name, Arity) removes all clauses of the predicate
Name/Arity from the database.
abolish/2 always succeeds.
@abolish/1
#abolish/1
abolish(Name) removes all clauses of all predicates with the
name ``Name'' and any arity from the database.
abolish/1 always succeeds.
@abolish/2
#abort/0
:abort/0 and restart/0
#restart/0
:abort/0 and restart/0
#abort/0 and restart/0
abort/0 raises an uncatchable error that interrupts program
execution. The error message
``ERROR 1: execution aborted'' is printed.

restart/0 works like abort/0, but prints no error
message.
@abort/0
#abort/2
abort/2 makes it possible to raise an error caught with error/2
after all (uncatchably). A clause of the form:

        error(X,Y) :- abort(X,Y).

works as if error/2 were not defined at all. The error that was
so laboriously caught is raised after all.
@error/2
#ancestors/1
ancestors/1 unifies its argument with a list of the calls
that led from the toplevel to the current call. This allows
a kind of after-the-fact debugging.
#arg/3
arg(N, Term, SubTerm) is used for the analysis and synthesis
of terms. arg/3 unifies ``SubTerm'' with the
``N''-th subterm of ``Term''.
Example:

?- arg( 2 , hello(first, second(Y)) , X).
X = second(Y).
@functor/3
@=../2
#argc/1
argc/1 unifies its argument with the value of the
parameter argc of the C function main of the Prolog system.
Together with argv/2 this gives access to the command line
parameters of the Prolog system.
@argv/2
#argv/2
argv(Parameter, N) unifies ``Parameter'' with the ``N''-th
parameter of the command line HU-Prolog was called with.
Parameter zero is (as in C) the name of the program.

WARNING: The order of the arguments may change
         soon!
@argc/1
#ttyask/1
:ask/1
#ask/1
ask(ASCII_VALUE) reads a line from the current input stream.
Then the ASCII value of the first character of the line is
unified with the argument of ask/1. When reading past the end
of the file, the ASCII value -1 is used.

ttyask/1 works the same way, but reads from 'stdin'
@get/1
@get0/1
#asserta/1
:assert/1
#assertz/1
:assert/1
#assert/1
asserta(Clause) adds the clause ``Clause'' to the database before
all other clauses with the same principal functor.

assertz(Clause) adds the clause ``Clause'' to the database after
all other clauses with the same principal functor.

assert(Clause) :- assertz(Clause).
#assign/2
assign(Stream_Name, Stream_Description) defines a new stream
with the name ``Stream_Name''. Stream_Description can be
one of the following:
a) an atom
    a1) the name of an already existing stream.
        => the new stream is connected to the old one.
    a2) any other atom
        => the new stream is assigned to a file with the name
           of the atom.
b) a structure: window(X,Y,XL,YL,Name,PropertyList)
    => a window is assigned to the stream.
       It has `X' and `Y' as the coordinates of its upper left
       corner, `XL' and `YL' as the size of the window,
       Name as the name shown in its frame, and `PropertyList'
       as an (implementation-specific!) list of properties
       of this window.

@Stream Operations
@Windows
@window/0
@Properties of the Local Installation
#atom/1
atom(X) is true if its argument is an atom.
#atomic/1
Definition:

    atomic(X) :- atom(X).
    atomic(X) :- number(X).
@atom/1
@number/1
#number/1
Definition:

    number(N) :- integer(N).
    number(N) :- real(N).
@real/1
@integer/1
#integer/1
integer(N) is true exactly if `N' is an integer.
#real/1
real(R) is true exactly if `R' is a real number.
#compound/1
Definition:

    compound(X) :- not atomic(X) , not var(X).
@var/1
@atomic/1
@not/1
#var/1
var(X) is true exactly if its argument is an unbound
variable.
#nonvar/1
Definition:

    nonvar(X) :- not var(X).
@var/1
@not/1
#ground/1
ground( Term ) is true exactly if the argument contains no
free variables.
#invar/1
Definition:
    invar(X) :-
        not ground(X).

WARNING: This predicate may be removed in later
         versions.
@ground/1
@not/1
#list/1
Definition:

    list(X)  :-
        var(X), ! fail.
    list([]).
    list([_|T]) :-
        list(T).

@string/1
#string/1
Definition:

    string(X)  :-
        var(X), ! fail.
    string([]).
    string([Val|T]) :-
        (Val >= 32 , Val =< 127), % or (Val >= 0 , Val =< 255)
        string(T).

@list/1
#call/1
Definition:

    call(X) :- X.

call/1 is NOT transparent to !/0.
@!/0
#clause/2
clause(Head, Body) searches the database for a clause whose
head unifies with `Head' and whose body unifies with `Body'.
Facts have the body `true'. The argument `Head' must be
instantiated at least far enough to determine the principal
functor of the clause.
#close/1
close(Open_Stream) closes the stream `Open_Stream'.
If it is the current standard input and/or standard output,
these are set to the streams `stdin' and `stdout'
respectively.
#open/1
open(Stream) opens the given stream for reading and writing.
Unlike `see/1' and `tell/1', the current input or output
stream is not changed.
#see/1
see(Stream) opens the given stream for reading and makes it
the current input stream.
#seeing/1
seeing(X) unifies its argument with the name of the current
input stream.
#seen/0
seen closes the current input stream. Afterwards
`stdin' becomes the new current input stream.
#tell/1
tell(Stream) opens the given stream for writing and makes it
the current output stream.
#telling/1
telling(X) unifies its argument with the name of the current
output stream.
#told/0
told closes the current output stream. Afterwards `stdout'
becomes the current output stream.

#ttycls/0
:cls/0
#cls/0
cls/0 clears the screen / the window that represents the current
output stream. If that is not a screen or window, the result
is undefined. (Expect the escape sequence for clearing the
screen to be written to the current output stream.)

ttycls/0 works like cls/0, but always on 'stdout'
#ttygotoxy/2
:gotoxy/2
#gotoxy/2
gotoxy(X,Y) positions the cursor on the screen / in the window
that represents the current output stream. If that is not a
screen or window, the result is undefined.
(Expect the escape sequence for cursor positioning to be
written to the current output stream.)

ttygotoxy/2 works like gotoxy/2, but always on 'stdout'
#consult/1
consult(Stream) opens the given stream and reads it in.
Clauses defined in the stream are added to the database
with assertz/1.

If the atom `end/0' is read or the predicate `end/0' is
executed, reading of the stream stops early.

As an abbreviation, list notation is allowed.
    [ F ] :- consult(F).
    [ F1 , F2 | T ] :- consult(F1) , [ F2 | T ].

@assertz/1
@reconsult/1
#reconsult/1
reconsult/1 works like consult/1, but all clauses to be
redefined are first removed from the database.
@consult/1

#current_atom/1
A call current_atom(Name / Arity) unifies Name and Arity with the
name and arity of an atom known to the interpreter.
On backtracking the predicate returns all atoms currently
known to the system.
@current_op/3
@current_predicate/1
#current_predicate/1
current_predicate(Name/Arity) unifies its argument on backtracking
with all predicates known to the interpreter.
@current_atom/1
@current_op/3
#current_op/3
A call current_op(V,A,N) unifies V, A and N with the precedence,
the associativity and the name of an operator known to the system.
New solutions are generated on backtracking.
The following program lists all operators known to the
interpreter.

    list_op     :-
        current_op(V,A,N),
        write((?-op(V,A,N)),nl,
        fail.
@op/3
@current_atom/1
@current_predicate/1
#dict/1
:dict/1 and sdict/1
#sdict/1
:dict/1 and sdict/1
#dict/1 and sdict/1
sdict(X) unifies its argument with the list of all atoms
predefined by the system, dict/1 with the list of all
user-defined atoms.

These predicates exist only for historical reasons and should
no longer be used. Alternatives are current_atom/1,
current_predicate/1 and sys/1.
@current_atom/1
@current_predicate/1
@sys/1
#!/0
If you don't know what the cut does in Prolog, this online
help is too short for you. I recommend a good Prolog book.
@Literature
#Literature
When in doubt, first read the HU-Prolog user manual.
If you don't have it, please request it from the author.

The standard introductory book is still

    Clocksin/Mellish: Programming in Prolog, Springer Verlag 81

Of the more advanced books, I like:

Tore Amble: Logic Programming and Knowledge Engineering
            Addison Wesley 87

Unfortunately I cannot list all the other 365 Prolog books
here. (Many of them really aren't worth it either.)
@Contacting the Author
#Database Operations
The following predicates affect the contents of the
Prolog database.
@assert/1
@asserta/1
@assertz/1
@retract/1
@retractall/1
@abolish/1
@abolish/2
@consult/1
@reconsult/1
@clause/2
#date/3
date(Day, Month, Year) unifies its arguments with the
current date.
@time/3
@weekday/1
#time/3
time(Hour, Minute, Second) unifies its arguments with
the current time.
@date/3
@weekday/1
#weekday/1
weekday(Day) unifies its argument with the number of the current
day of the week. [ Monday == 1 , ... , Sunday == 7 ]
@time/3
@date/3
#display/1
display(Term) writes its argument to the current output stream.
The term is ALWAYS written in normal form, that is, without
taking operator declarations into account.
That is not very readable for the user, but it is for the
Prolog system. A term written with display/1 should always
be readable with read/1.
@read/1
@write/1
@writeq/1
#ttywrite/1
:write/1
#write/1
write(Term) writes its argument to the current output stream.
The operator declarations currently in effect are used to
make the output as readable as possible.

ttywrite/1 works the same way, but always writes to 'stdout'
@writeq/1
@display/1
#writeq/1
writeq(Term) writes its argument to the current output stream.
Atoms are enclosed in quotes where necessary. Unlike with
write/1, they can therefore always be read back in. Unlike
display/1, however, writeq/1 uses the current operator
declarations to make the output readable.
@write/1
@display/1
#echo/1
echo( on )  switches echo mode on.
echo( off ) switches echo mode off.
echo( X )   unifies its argument with `on' or `off', depending
            on whether echo mode is currently on or off.

Echo mode means that EVERY character read by the Prolog system
is echoed to the standard output.
@log/1
#log/1
log( on )  switches log mode on.
log( off ) switches log mode off.
log( X )   unifies its argument with `on' or `off', depending
            on whether log mode is currently on or off.

Log mode means that ALL input and output of the interpreter is
recorded in a log file. This file must be specified when the
Prolog system is started.
@echo/1
@Command Line Options
#fileerrors/1
fileerrors( on )        switches this mode on.
fileerrors( off )       switches this mode off.
fileerrors( X )         unifies its argument with `on' or `off'
                        depending on whether the mode is on or
                        off.

If fileerror mode is on, the stream handling predicates
(open/1, close/1, ....) raise an error when they do not
succeed.

If the mode is off, they only cause backtracking.
#end/0
end tells the interpreter that the current toplevel should be
terminated once the current computation has finished.

The following count as toplevels:
- the built-in interactive toplevel
- a user-defined toplevel defined via toplevel/0
- a consult/1 that is currently running
@toplevel/0
