#functor/3
functor(Term,Name,Arity) decomposes the term given as the first
argument into the name ``Name'' and the arity
``Arity'' of its principal functor.

Example:

?- functor(hello(world),X,Y).
X = hello
Y = 1
?- functor(X,f,3).
X = f(V1,V2,V3)
@arg/3
@=../2
#name/2
name(Atom, String) decomposes atoms into the characters that
represent the name of the atom, and vice versa.

Example:

?- name(hello,X).
X = ""hello""
?- name(17,X).
X = ""17""        % so it works with numbers too.
?- name(X, ""17"").
X = '17'        % THE ATOM '17', NOT THE NUMBER 17.
#ttyget/1
:get/1
#get/1
get(ASCII) :-
    repeat,
        get0(Value),
    (Value == -1 ; Value > 32 , Value < 128),
    !,
    ASCII = Value.

ttyget/1 works like get, but reads from 'stdin'
@get0/1
@ask/1
#ttyget0/1
:get0/1
#get0/1
get0(ASCII) reads a character from the current input stream and
unifies the ASCII value of that character with the argument of
get0/1. At the end of the file the ASCII value -1 is used.

ttyget0/1 works like get0/1, but reads from 'stdin'
@ask/1
@get/1
#getenv/2
getenv(Value, Name) reads the environment variable `Name' and
unifies `Value' with its value.

WARNING: The order of the arguments may change
         soon!
@putenv/2
#putenv/2
putenv(Name, Value) sets the environment variable `Name'
to the value `Value'.
@getenv/2
#help/0
:help
#help
help calls this online help. The top line shows the name of
the current topic.
After a help text (like this one) has been printed, the
references reachable from this help text are listed
(see below).

Then a number or a command is expected.
- the number 0 exits the help system
- any other number goes to the correspondingly numbered
  reference
- the string `..' goes to the previous item; if there is
  none, the help system is exited.
- the string `.' shows the current help text again.
- any other string is taken as a new topic, which is
  displayed (if it exists).
@HU-Prolog Online Help
#hide/1
This predicate expects an atom or a list of atoms as its argument.
hide/1 marks the end of the scope of these atoms. The given atoms
that were visible so far get a new name, and the atoms previously
hidden with private/1 become visible again.
@private/1
#private/1
The predicate private/1 expects either an atom or a list of atoms
as its argument. These atoms are declared local. As in classical
programming languages, a new declaration level is created.
These declaration levels can be nested. If an atom with one of
these names is now created (this always happens in read/1 and
name/2), it is different from the atoms of the same name created
before the call to private/1.
@hide/1
#interrupt/0
If the predicate interrupt/0 is defined by the user, it is
called every time the signal SIGINT is raised.
#not/1

Definition:

    not(X) :- call(X) , ! , fail.
    not(_).

@call/1
@!/0
@fail/0
@\+/1
#\+/1

Definition:

    \+(X) :- call(X) , ! , fail.
    \+(_).

This predicate is equivalent to not/1 and exists in HU-Prolog
only because some strange Prolog dialects and the emerging
standard want it that way.

@call/1
@!/0
@fail/0
@not/1
#ttyput/1
:put/1
#put/1
put(N) expects as its argument either a number or a list of
numbers. Each number is interpreted as an ASCII value, and the
corresponding character is written to the current output
stream.

ttyput/1 works the same way, but always writes to 'stdout'

Example:

?- put(65).
A
yes
?- put(""hello""). % ""hello"" = [104, 101, 108, 108, 111]
hello
yes
#ttytab/1
:tab/1
#tab/1
Definition:

    tab(N) :- N =< 0,!.
    tab(N) :-
        put(32),        % a space
        N1 is N -1,
        tab(N1).

ttytab/1 works the same way, but always writes to 'stdout'
@put/1
@is/2
