:Debugging
#occur_check/1
occur_check/1 controls the state of the occur check flag

The occur check tests, when a variable X is bound to a term T,
whether the variable X occurs in T. By the definition of
unification, X and T are not unifiable in that case. Since this
test is relatively time-consuming, it is usually not performed.

        ?- occur_check(on).
        ?- X = a(X).
        no
        ?- occur_check(off).
        ?- X = a(X).
        X = a(a(a(a(a(a( ...... ))))).
        yes

@Flags
#op/3
op/3 makes one or more new operators known to the interpreter.
In a call `op(P , A , O )', P is the priority, A the
associativity and O the name, or a list of names, of the
operators to be defined.

Numbers in the range 1 - 1200 (2000) are allowed for P. A higher
priority *separates* more strongly (it does not bind more strongly).
If 0 is given as the priority, the operator declaration is
removed.

The values `xf' and `yf' are allowed for A for postfix operators,
`fx' and `fy' for prefix operators, and `xfx', `xfy' and `yfx'
for infix operators.

The operators currently defined in the system can be obtained
with `current_op/3'.

For the purpose of operators, consult a Prolog book.
@current_op/3
@Literature
#ttyread/1
:read/1
#read/1
read(X) reads a term from the current input stream and
unifies it with `X'. At the end of the file the term `end'
is returned.

ttyread/1 works like read/1, but reads from 'stdin'
@read/2
#read/2
read(Term, VarList) works like read/1, but additionally
returns a list of the external variable names in the
second argument.

Example:
        ?- read(Term,VarList).
        hello(A).
        Term = hello(_31)
        VarList = [ 'A' = _31 ]
        yes
@read/1
#repeat/0
repeat/0 always succeeds (also on backtracking) and is thus
used to implement infinite loops.

Definition:

        repeat.
        repeat :- repeat.

WARNING: Since HU-Prolog does no tail recursion optimization,
         repeat/0 is implemented differently internally.
         A user-defined predicate of the form:

             r.
             r :- r.

         is semantically equivalent to repeat/0, but can
         (e.g. in `` ?- r, fail. '') lead to memory
         problems.

@Literature
@Flow Control
#restore/1
restore loads memory dumps created with save/1.
For this the Prolog system is restarted.

Definition:

        restore(Dump_File_Name) :-
                argv(PROLOG,0),
                system([PROLOG,'-r',Dump_File_Name]),
                halt.

@argv/2
@system/1
@halt/0
@Command Line Options
@save/1
#retract/1
retract/1 removes the clause given as its argument from the
database. The argument must at least determine the name and
the arity of the clause head.
On backtracking further clauses are removed -- but see the
WARNING below for an important restriction when the argument
is given explicitly as ``(Head :- Body)''.
Example (argument as a bare head, without ``:-''):

Let the database contain:
        b(1).
        b(2).
        b(3).
Then:
        ?- retract(b(X)).
        X = 1 ;
        X = 2 ;
        X = 3 ;
        no

WARNING: If the argument is given explicitly as
         ``(Head :- Body)'', retract/1 can ONLY match
         clauses with a real body (that is, rules), never
         facts -- regardless of backtracking. Internally a
         fact is stored without a body (not with the body
         `true'), and retract/1 then compares only the bare
         head, not ``Head :- true''. (clause/2 hides this
         difference from its users and returns Body=true
         for facts -- retract/1 does not.)
         Example:

         Let the database contain:
                 a(1) :- fail.
                 a(2).
         Then:
                 ?- retract(( a(X) :- Y )).
                 X = 1
                 Y = fail
                 no          % a(2) is NOT found,
                              % although it should match!

         Workaround: either give the argument without ``:-''
         (then it matches both facts and rules, see the
         b/1 example above), or search separately for facts
         (bare head) and rules (``Head :- Body'') as needed.
@retractall/1
@abolish/1
@abolish/2
@Database Operations
#retractall/1
Definition:
        retractall( X ) :-
            retract( X ),
            fail.
        retractall( _ ).

@retract/1
@abolish/1
@abolish/2
@Database Operations
#save/1
save(Filename) saves the current contents of the database
in the file named `Filename'.
This memory dump can later be loaded with restore/1, or
directly when starting the Prolog system.

On UNIX systems this memory dump is self-starting, that is,
it can be run as a program.
@restore/1
@Command Line Options
#ttyskip/1
:skip/1
#skip/1
The argument of skip/1 must be an arithmetic expression that
evaluates to an integer value.
skip/1 reads characters from the current input stream until
the ASCII value of a character read is unifiable with the value
of the argument of skip/1, or the end of the file is reached.

ttyskip/1 works the same way, but always reads from 'stdin'
@ask/1
@get0/1
@get/1
