#true/0
Heh, this is *THE* Prolog predicate.

Definition:

        true.

But that was obvious, wasn't it?
If not, I recommend an introductory book on Prolog!
@Literature
#version/0
version/0 shows the version number and some other
information.
#unget/0
unget puts the last character read back into the current
input stream. Only one character can be put back at a
time.

Usage:
Skip all spaces in the current input stream:

        skip_spaces :- repeat, get0(CH) , [CH] \= "" "", unget.

The non-space character read is put back into the current
input stream.
#unknown/1
If the predicate unknown/1 has been defined by the user, it
is called whenever the Prolog system finds no clauses for a
call that is about to be executed.

unknown/1 receives the current call as its argument. This
makes intelligent error handling possible, such as
loading predicates dynamically.

Example:

        unknown(X) :-
            load_unknown_predicate(X),
            call(X).    % call X again

@ensure/3
#seek/2
seek(File , Position) changes the position of the
read/write pointer in the given file.

The first argument of seek/2 must be the name of an already
opened regular file (that is, not a window or a
screen).

If the second argument of seek/2 is a variable, it is
unified with the current position of the read/write
pointer.
If the second argument is the atom end/0, the read/write
pointer is positioned at the end of the file.
If the second argument is a non-negative number, the
read/write pointer is positioned relative to the start of the file.
If the second argument is a negative number, the
read/write pointer is positioned relative to the end of the file.
@Stream Operations
#timer/1
timer/1 sets and queries a running timer. It measures the
time used by the system in hundredths of a second.

If the argument of timer/1 is a number, the timer is set
to that value.

If the argument of timer/1 is a variable, it is unified
with the current value of the timer.

Example:

 :- timer(0), test, timer(N),
    write('The call of test took '), write(N),
    write('/100 seconds').

@time/3
@date/3
@weekday/1
#system/1
system/1 calls another program.

The argument of system/1 must be a list of atoms. These
are put together into a call of an external program and
passed to the C function system().

Example:

 :-system([ls,'-la']).

calls the program 'ls' with the argument '-la'.

BUG:
        At the moment the same can be achieved with
            :- system(['ls -la']).
#toplevel/0
HU-Prolog has a built-in toplevel, which

1) prints a prompt (""?- "")
2) reads a term
3) calls this term
4) prints the resulting variable bindings, and
   asks the user whether more solutions are wanted.

Since one sometimes wants to do something else, there is the
predicate toplevel/0. If it is defined, it is called
instead of the built-in toplevel.
@prompt/0
#prompt/0
If the predicate prompt/0 is defined, it is called by the
built-in toplevel. The prompt ""?- "" is then not
printed.

Example:

    prompt :- put(""hello you> "").

@toplevel/0
#window/0
window/0 is true exactly if the window system can be
used.
