#Unifiability
The predicate =/2 is true exactly if the two terms given as
arguments are unifiable.

The predicate \=/2 is its negation.

Definition:

        X = X.

        X \= Y :-
                not X = Y.
#Identity
:==/2
#Lexicographic Order
The predicates @</2, @=</2, @>/2, @>=/2, @=/2 and @\=/2 compare
two terms by their lexicographic order. It is defined as
follows:

- Variables are smaller than all other terms and equal to each other
- Numbers are smaller than all other non-variable terms and
  are ordered among themselves by their numeric value
- Structured terms are ordered by their principal functors and,
  if these are equal, by their arguments according to their
  lexicographic order.

For example:

        X @= Y          X @< 1          1 @< 2.5
        a @< b          a(1) @< b       a @< a(1)

#Analysis and Synthesis of Atoms
@name/2
@current_atom/1
#warn/1
warn/1 controls the warnings generated by the system.
The following values are allowed as arguments of warn/1:
1) the atom 'on' : all warnings are switched on.
2) the atom 'off': all warnings are switched off
3) a list of the atoms: 'op' , 'retract' , 'consult'
   'listing', 'spy' and/or 'read'. This switches on the
   warnings generated by the corresponding predicates.
   All warnings not given in the list are switched
   off.
4) a variable: it is unified with the list of currently
   active warnings.

All warnings are written to the stream 'stdwarn'.
@op/3
@retract/1
@consult/1
@listing/1
@spy/1
@read/1
@Predefined Streams
#Predefined Streams
HU-Prolog knows the following predefined streams:

stdin : the standard input of the interpreter
stdout: the standard output of the interpreter
stderr: the standard error output of the interpreter. All
        errors are reported here.
stdwarn: normally the standard output of the interpreter.
        All warnings are reported here.
stdhelp: normally the standard output of the interpreter.
        The dialog of the predicate help/0 takes place
        here.

#sys/1

sys( Name / Arity ) is true exactly if the atom denoted by
Name/Arity is defined as a 'system atom'. This means that
any modification of predicates with this atom as their
principal functor is normally forbidden.

More precisely, changes are only allowed in 'system mode'.
This is in effect while the 'system file' given by the
command line option '-s' (or by default the file named
.prologrc) is being processed.

@sysmode/1
@Command Line Options
#wget0/1

The predicate wget0/1 reads a character from the current input
stream and unifies its ASCII value with the argument of wget0/1.
(So identical to get0/1)

The difference from get0/1 is the behavior of wget0/1 when
the current input stream is a window.
Input then happens without echo and without
waiting for the end-of-line marker.

@get0/1
@window/0
#isatty/1

This predicate may be thrown out again !!

isatty/1 is true if the output stream given as its argument
is the screen (or another device).
#sysmode/1

sysmode(X) sets (with 'on' or 'off') the value of the
'sysmode flag', or queries it (if the argument is a
variable).

The sysmode flag is normally active while the system file
given by the command line option '-s' is being processed.
Afterwards it is set to 'off'.

This flag controls whether built-in predicates can be
modified.

That should not be done without a deeper understanding
of Prolog.
