:login/0 and logout/0
#Stream Concept
At any time HU-Prolog has exactly one current input stream
and one current output stream. Streams as logical objects
can be assigned to files, to the standard streams of the
interpreter, or to windows.

The assignment of a stream to a physical object is done with
the predicate assign/2.

@assign/2
#Windows
Working with windows is implementation-specific.

It is important that the HU-Prolog system must be started with
the special command line option '-x' to make working with
windows possible at all. Furthermore, windows should be
assigned to the standard streams with 'assign/2'.

@Command Line Options
@window/0
@assign/2
@Example .prologrc File for Working with Windows
#Example .prologrc File for Working with Windows

Assuming a 25 * 80 screen:

?- window,
    assign(tracewin,window(1,18,109,21,' Trace ',[])),
    assign(errwin ,window(10,10,80,20,' Error ',[])),
    assign(stdwin, window(1,2,109,15,' HU-Prolog ',[])),
    assign(stdhelp,window(1,28,109,12,' HU-Prolog Help ',[])),
    assign(stdtrace,tracewin),
    assign(stderr,errwin),
    assign(stdin, stdwin),
    assign(stdout,stdwin)
  ;true.

@window/0
@assign/2
@Predefined Streams
#Command Line Options
HU-Prolog knows the following options:

-[dD] :            not meant for the user. This option
                   switches the debug flag on ($debug/1)
-[lL] <filename> : sets the name of the log file. When the
                   log flag is on, all input and output of
                   the interpreter is recorded in this file.
                   -l leaves the log flag 'off',
                   -L switches it 'on'
-[wW] :            switches the warn flag 'on'
-[vV] :            -v suppresses some output of the
                   interpreter. This allows HU-Prolog to
                   be used as a filter in a pipe in
                   principle.
-[oO] :            switches the occur_check flag 'on'
-[xX] :            enables working with windows
-[sS] <filename> : sets the name of the system file. If not
                   given, '.prologrc' or '~/.prologrc' is
                   used. -S also prevents loading the
                   global prologrc file.

-[rR] <filename> : loads a memory dump created with save/1
@Windows
@Flags
#sort/2
:sort/2 and sort0/2
#sort0/2
:sort/2 and sort0/2
#sort/2 and sort0/2

These predicates sort a list of terms given as the first
argument and unify the result with the second
argument.
Sorting is done according to the relation @</2

Unlike sort/2, sort0/2 removes all duplicate
entries.

Example:

?- sort(""abccba"",X).
X = ""aabbcc""
?- sort0(""abccbd"",X).
X = ""abcd""

WARNING: These predicates may be removed some day.

@@</2
#exit/1 , halt/0, end/0

These predicates terminate the Prolog system.

@halt/0
@end/0
@exit/1
#Arithmetic Comparison Operators
The predicates </2, =</2 , =:=/2 , =\=/2 , >=/2 and >/2
first evaluate their arguments (using :=/2).
The results of this evaluation must be numeric values.
These are then compared and (depending on the result of the
comparison) cause the predicate to succeed or
fail.

@is/2
@:=/2
