#Standard Functions and Operations
Any necessary conversion of the argument from int to real
happens automatically.
@0-ary Functions
@1-ary Functions
@2-ary Functions
#0-ary Functions
Name     | Type | Meaning
---------+------+--------------------------------------------
maxint   | int  | largest integer
minint   |      | smallest integer
---------+------+-------------------------------------------
e        | real | 2.718281828...
pi       |      | 3.1415.....
---------+------+-------------------------------------------
maxarity | int  | maximum functor arity
maxdepth | int  | maximum recursion depth when processing terms

@1-ary Functions
@2-ary Functions
#1-ary Functions
Name      | Type         |  Meaning
----------+--------------+---------------------------------
-x        | int -> int   |  arithmetic negation
          | real -> real |
----------+--------------+---------------------------------
/x        | int -> int   |  logical negation
~x        |              |  bitwise negation
----------+--------------+---------------------------------
real(x)   | int -> real  |  type conversion
----------+--------------+---------------------------------
entier(x) | real -> int  |

In addition there are the following 1-ary functions of type
real -> real from the standard C library, adopted into
HU-Prolog with the same meaning:

exp, ln, log10, sqrt, sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan,
floor and ceil
@0-ary Functions
@2-ary Functions
#2-ary Functions
Name    |    Type           | Meaning
--------+-------------------+------------------------------
B << N  | int*int -> int    | bitwise shift
B >> N  |                   |
A && B  |                   | logical conjunction
A \\ B  |                   | logical disjunction (|| in C)
A & B   |                   | bitwise conjunction
A \ B   |                   | bitwise disjunction (| in C)
--------+-------------------+------------------------------
B ** E  | real*int -> real  | power function
--------+-------------------+------------------------------
A + B   | int*int -> int    |
A - B   | real*real -> real |
A * B   |                   |
--------+-------------------+------------------------------
A / B   | real*real -> real | real division
--------+-------------------+------------------------------
A // B  | int*int -> int    | integer division
A mod B |                   | modulo (in C: A % B)
@0-ary Functions
@1-ary Functions
#Arithmetic
:Arithmetic and Functional Programming
#login/0 and logout/0
The predicates login/0 and logout/0 can be defined by the
user, to be executed exactly once each.

Before HU-Prolog switches to interactive mode, the
predicate login/0 is executed exactly once.

After HU-Prolog leaves interactive mode, the
predicate logout/0 is executed exactly once.
@Global Control of System Behavior
#Global Variables and User-Defined Functions
Global variables and user-defined functions are an
extension of HU-Prolog that does not exist in this form in
other dialects, but could be defined there.

An (N+1)-ary predicate is treated as an N-ary
function. The first argument of the predicate
represents the result.

Example:

        fac(1 , 0).
        fac(R , N) :-
                N1 := N -1 ,
                R := N * fac(N1).
        ?- X := fac(10).
        X = 3628800

If the evaluation of the predicate fails, the calling term
becomes the result of the evaluation:

        X := hello(world).
        WARNING: no clause for relation hello/2
        X = hello(world).
@:=/2
@is/2
#Debugging
trace/1 switches trace mode for all predicates on or off,
depending on its argument, or queries it.
spy(P) and nospy(P) switch trace mode on or off selectively
for the given predicate.

trace :- trace(on).
notrace :- trace(off).

For more information please read the HU-Prolog user
manual.
#stats/0
stats/0 gives statistics on the current use of the
various memory areas of HU-Prolog.
#Properties of the Local Installation

Please look at the file '/usr/local/lib/huprolog/prologrc'.

To prevent this file from being loaded, use the -S
option when starting the system.
